      sources: "Sources: Learn, List groups; List schedules; List policy assignments; Create requests; Update rule; Create group; group resource. Chapter 05, “Least privilege per call.”",
      say: `Who is this for? Developers building the write path, the identity engineers who own the groups and the file, and the I S S O's who review each plan before it's applied. Then, what the code may do. Every call gets its least-privileged permission. The read side needs four permissions and none of them can write. The plan lists exactly the permissions its own changes need, so the app that applies it holds those and no more. And the one powerful permission, the one that can assign any directory role, is kept off the pipeline entirely: create these groups by hand.`,
      detail: `Audience: developers who build the project's write path for privileged groups, the identity engineers who own the PRV groups, and the ISSOs who review the changes. You should read PowerShell 7 and JSON, and know what an eligible assignment is. You don't need a tenant until Lab 2. Least privilege per call: List groups (isAssignableToRole, PRV-*): Group.ReadBasic.All; Learn's table names Group-NestingSupport.ReadWrite.All as least privileged, a write permission, so the code takes the first read-only one (research gaps, row 52). List eligibilitySchedules: PrivilegedEligibilitySchedule.Read.AzureADGroup; delegated Global Reader or Privileged Role Administrator for role-assignable groups. List assignmentSchedules: PrivilegedAssignmentSchedule.Read.AzureADGroup; same. List roleManagementPolicyAssignments (groups): RoleManagementPolicy.Read.AzureADGroup. Create eligibilityScheduleRequest: PrivilegedEligibilitySchedule.ReadWrite.AzureADGroup; Privileged Role Administrator for role-assignable groups. Create assignmentScheduleRequest: PrivilegedAssignmentSchedule.ReadWrite.AzureADGroup; same. Update unifiedRoleManagementPolicyRule (groups): RoleManagementPolicy.ReadWrite.AzureADGroup; Privileged Role Administrator, per the how-to. Create group (role-assignable): Group.Create, and RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory to set isAssignableToRole; at least Privileged Role Administrator. The read task needs the first four and nothing that ends in ReadWrite. The plan lists the permissions its own operations need, so the app that applies it holds those and no more. Design guidance: create the PRV groups by hand, as a Privileged Role Administrator, and keep RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory off the pipeline's app; the code only plans the creation. One Learn statement needs Lab 2: the role-assignable groups page says managing their membership through Graph needs RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory, while the PIM for Groups request pages name only the AzureADGroup permissions (research gaps, row 53).`,
      cite: ["pa05-group-list", "pa05-elig-list", "pa05-asg-list", "pa05-pa-list", "pa05-elig-req", "pa05-asg-req", "pa05-rule-update", "pa05-rules-howto", "pa05-group-create", "pa05-group-resource", "pa05-rag"],
